---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="The Outcome-Linked Transfer Framework — Shaurya Vijay Sinha"
  description="A pitch for restructuring India's intergovernmental transfers around measurable outcomes, built for the National Policy Hackathon at IIM Calcutta and IIFT Kolkata."
>
  <article class="section section--tight reveal">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Fiscal federalism · National Policy Hackathon</p>
      <h1>The Outcome-Linked Transfer Framework</h1>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem; font-style:italic; color:var(--body-text); margin-top:-0.4em;">
        A pitch deck built for the National Policy Hackathon hosted by IIM
        Calcutta and IIFT Kolkata, on fiscal policy and multi-level governance.
      </p>

      <div class="key-finding">
        <p class="key-finding__label">Result</p>
        <p class="key-finding__figure">National Finalist</p>
        <p class="key-finding__context">
          Competed in the finals against teams from MBA and PhD programmes,
          presenting as Team Equi-Nomics.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        India's intergovernmental transfer system carries two structural
        imbalances. Vertically, the Union controls most of the tax pool —
        states receive only 41% of it through the 15th Finance Commission's
        tax devolution formula — while transfers go on to bridge roughly 80%
        of states' fiscal gap and make up around 36% of state revenue. States
        end up with little incentive to grow their own tax base when transfers
        already cover most of the shortfall.
      </p>
      <p>
        Horizontally, the transfers that do arrive aren't well-targeted. Own
        source revenue finances only 46–58% of state expenditure, with wide
        disparities between states. Formula-based transfers create what we
        called a substitution effect: because grants fill deficits regardless
        of a state's own effort, they weaken the incentive to raise local
        revenue. And because grants are tied to input spending and
        infrastructure norms rather than measurable outcomes, there's no
        accountability loop connecting the money to what it actually achieves
        in education or health.
      </p>

      <h2>The proposal</h2>
      <p>
        We designed an Outcome-Linked Social Sector Transfer Framework that
        keeps the existing transfer pool — no new outlay — but changes how
        it's allocated. Each state's social sector grant combines a base
        equalisation floor with a population-weighted deprivation adjustment,
        scaled by a Social Deprivation Index built from education, health, and
        living-standards indicators. That whole allocation is then scaled by a
        performance multiplier, bounded between 0.85 and 1.15, that rewards
        states for measurable year-on-year improvement in outcomes rather than
        for spending more.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second piece replaces the logic of the grant itself: instead of
        funding a state's <em>reported deficit</em> — which a state can
        widen simply by under-collecting its own revenue — the framework funds
        the gap between a normative minimum social-sector cost and the
        state's actual revenue capacity. That single change is what removes
        the substitution effect: a state can no longer grow its transfer by
        shrinking its own tax effort.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting there</h2>
      <p>
        We proposed a budget-neutral, three-year rollout rather than a single
        reform: pilot the Social Deprivation Index across 10–15% of grants in
        Year 1 using existing UDISE+ and NFHS data; activate the performance
        multiplier through the existing PFMS infrastructure in Year 2; and
        transition fully to the normative fiscal-gap model by Year 3. No
        constitutional changes are required — only a reallocation of how
        existing transfers are calculated — and the whole system stays
        auditable through the Ministry of Finance and CAG.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="section--tight reveal">
    <div class="wrap" style="display:flex; gap:var(--space-3); flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; border-top:1px solid var(--rule); padding-top:var(--space-3);">
      <p style="margin:0; max-width:32rem;">Download the full pitch deck — problem statement, gap analysis, the complete allocation formula, execution roadmap, and references.</p>
      <a class="btn btn--solid" href="/files/IIMC_IIFTK_Pitch.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Download the deck (.pdf)</a>
    </div>
  </section>

